---
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>3.6 (Day 1) — Combat Engine RPG Build: Attack &amp; Crit Resolver</h1>

<p>
    <strong>CED Topics 2.1, 2.3, 2.5 · 90-Minute Design-First RPG Build (Day 1 of 2).</strong> Your
    character has ability scores from Unit 2 and a <code>Dice</code> engine from Unit 1. Over the
    next two classes you will build <code>Combat.java</code> — the tactical combat resolver that
    powers every dungeon fight. Today you design and build Parts 1 and 2 (deterministic strike
    rules and live dice integration); next class (<code>3.6b</code>) you build damage mitigation
    and tactical stances.
</p>

<div class="callout">
    <strong>Design-First Workflow (Download Both Starter Files):</strong>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/Combat.java" download>Combat.java</a> — starter file with Javadocs,
            stubs, and a built-in <strong>Tactical Combat Engine Sandbox</strong> in
            <code>main()</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/Dice.java" download>Dice.java</a> — your dice engine from Unit 1.7.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:0.5rem; margin-bottom:0.25rem;">
        <strong>How to work today:</strong>
    </p>
    <ol style="margin-bottom:0.5rem;">
        <li><strong>Design before coding:</strong> Answer the Guided Design questions on
            deterministic rule testing, Natural 1/20 branch priority, and avoiding duplicated logic.</li>
        <li><strong>Draft &amp; test in the sandbox:</strong> Draft each method below, click
            <strong>Copy</strong> to paste into <code>Combat.java</code>, and test your combat log
            with:</li>
    </ol>
    <pre><code>javac Dice.java Combat.java
java Combat</code></pre>
    <p style="margin-top:0.4rem; margin-bottom:0;">
        <strong>Step 3 (Day 1 Verification):</strong> Unlock <code>CombatCheck.java</code> below
        and pass all <strong>14 checks in Parts 1 and 2</strong> on your first run (Parts 3–5 are
        tomorrow!).
    </p>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 1 · Deterministic Rule Evaluation</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~45 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 1: <code>evaluateStrike(int d20Roll, int attackBonus, int targetAC, int baseDamage, boolean canCrit)</code></h3>

    <div class="design-context">
        <h4>What This Function Does &amp; Why It Exists</h4>
        <p>
            Professional game engines—and AP CS A Free Response questions—separate <strong>random
            dice rolling</strong> from <strong>rule evaluation</strong>. Passing
            <code>d20Roll</code> and <code>baseDamage</code> as parameters lets us deterministically
            verify Natural 20 crits, Natural 1 fumbles, and exact AC ties without hoping luck strikes
            in 100 random runs.
        </p>
        <h4>5e Strike Rules &amp; Parameters</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li><code>canCrit == true</code> (Players &amp; Bosses): <code>d20Roll == 20</code>
                always hits for <code>baseDamage * 2</code>; <code>d20Roll == 1</code> always misses
                for <code>0</code> damage, regardless of <code>attackBonus</code> or <code>targetAC</code>.</li>
            <li>Standard attack (any other roll, or whenever <code>canCrit == false</code>): if
                <code>d20Roll + attackBonus &gt;= targetAC</code> ("meets it, beats it"), return
                <code>baseDamage</code>; otherwise return <code>0</code>.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Guided Program Design: <code>evaluateStrike</code></h3>
        <p>Think through branch order before writing any <code>if / else if</code>:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Natural 1 vs. Huge Bonus (Branch Order):</strong> Suppose a Level 15 Paladin
                has <code>attackBonus = 18</code> facing a Goblin with <code>targetAC = 10</code>,
                and rolls a Natural 1 (<code>d20Roll = 1</code>). Why must the
                <code>canCrit &amp;&amp; d20Roll == 20</code> and
                <code>canCrit &amp;&amp; d20Roll == 1</code> branches come <em>before</em>
                <code>d20Roll + attackBonus &gt;= targetAC</code>?</li>
            <li><strong>Monster Crit Guard:</strong> If a standard monster with
                <code>canCrit = false</code>, <code>attackBonus = 2</code>, and
                <code>targetAC = 15</code> rolls a <code>20</code>, what must
                <code>evaluateStrike(20, 2, 15, 6, false)</code> return, and what bug happens if
                you forget <code>canCrit &amp;&amp;</code> in the first branch?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Draft &amp; Test in <code>Combat.java</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write your complete <code>evaluateStrike</code> method body, copy it into
                <code>Combat.java</code>, and check Part 1 edge cases in <code>java Combat</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 2 · Live Dice Integration</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~45 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 2: <code>playerAttack(...)</code> &amp; <code>monsterAttack(...)</code></h3>

    <div class="design-context">
        <h4>What These Functions Do &amp; Why They Exist</h4>
        <p>
            These two methods connect your <code>Dice.java</code> utility to your
            <code>evaluateStrike</code> rule engine. By delegating the math to
            <code>evaluateStrike</code>, crit/fumble logic lives in exactly <strong>one place</strong>
            across the whole codebase.
        </p>
        <h4>Signatures &amp; Behavior</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li><code>public static int playerAttack(int attackBonus, int targetAC, int damageDie, String targetName)</code>
                — rolls <code>d20 = Dice.roll(20)</code> and <code>baseDmg = Dice.roll(damageDie)</code>,
                calls <code>evaluateStrike(d20, attackBonus, targetAC, baseDmg, true)</code>, prints
                a combat log line naming <code>targetName</code>, and returns damage dealt.</li>
            <li><code>public static int monsterAttack(int attackBonus, int targetAC, int damageDie, String monsterName)</code>
                — same steps, except calls <code>evaluateStrike(..., false)</code> (monsters cannot
                crit or auto-fumble).</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Guided Program Design: Single Source of Truth</h3>
        <p>Analyze modularity and probability edge cases:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Modularity:</strong> Why is calling <code>evaluateStrike</code> inside
                <code>playerAttack</code> and <code>monsterAttack</code> better design than copying
                the <code>if / else if</code> rules into both methods? If crits later change to
                <code>baseDamage * 3</code>, how many places do you edit?</li>
            <li><strong>Testing Randomness:</strong> How can a test call
                <code>playerAttack(0, 50, 6, "Dragon")</code> 250 times and still verify damage &gt; 0
                even though <code>20 + 0 &lt; 50</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Draft &amp; Test in <code>Combat.java</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write both <code>playerAttack</code> and <code>monsterAttack</code>, copy them into
                <code>Combat.java</code>, and test with <code>java Combat</code> before unlocking
                <code>CombatCheck.java</code> below?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Day 1 Milestone — Done When</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Parts 1 and 2 work cleanly in <code>java Combat</code></li>
            <li>You unlock <code>CombatCheck.java</code> below and pass all <strong>14 checks in Parts 1 and 2</strong> (Parts 3–5 are tomorrow!)</li>
            <li>You have downloaded your <code>.json</code> answers file for Canvas</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-03/combat-system.html">Student RPG Guide: Combat System</a></li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-03/relational-operators.html">Relational Operators</a></li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-03/boolean-logic.html">Boolean Logic</a></li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
